C343-131 - Unclear.  "The soldier swings her cockatrice corpse.  The
           soldier misses.  You smile at the soldier engagingly.
           You steal:  A - 5 cockatrice corpses.  You turn to
           stone..."  The greatest awkwardness comes from reporting
           the inventory letter, but there's a good reason for that.
           Perhaps the bug refers to the taboo against using a colon
           immediately after a verb in formal writing, but any
           context that formal would also not allow use of the
           second person ("You").  Perhaps "to" is not the ideal
           preposition, but it's not incorrect either.
C343-132 - Unknown.  With a loadstone, "Thump!  Ouch!  That hurts!
           You die..."  Kicking a cockatrice corpse, "You kick the
           cockatrice corpse with your bare feet.  You turn to
           stone..."  If polymorphed, "feet" can be e.g., "rear
           claws", "rear hooves"; perhaps "with your bare rear
           [whatevers]" is what is meant?  Dunno.
C343-133 - Unclear.  How is this different from C343-75 and/or
           C343-76?
C343-134 - Unknown.  I can't figure out how to actually engulf
           a cockatrice (as opposed to just biting it, which
           neither provides nutrition nor violates foodless).
C343-135 - Confirmed.  The messages when you first enter certain
           levels are written assuming that the character can see.
           The Archaeologist and Caveman and Tourist and Wizard
           quests all have issues.  Also, a couple of the quests
           assume you can smell (but I don't happen to know if it's
           possible to polyself into anything that can't, so maybe
           that's okay).  The Rogue quest blithely assumes you have
           free hands and to make a guild sign.
C343-136 - Fixed in NetHack 4 (credited to Patric Muller)
C343-137 - demon_talk assumes the player is conscious (otherwise,
           how could he be talking to anything), but dochug (which
           is seriously misnamed) calls it without checking that.
C343-138 - Confirmed, if they're tame.  Neither hostile spheres nor
           the player polyselfed into a sphere can reach the floor,
           but pet (e.g., flaming, shocking) spheres can and do.
C343-139 - Confirmed, playing passtune repeatedly on a flute.
           The first time, you get feedback no matter what.
           Subsequently, you get feedback if you can see or if
           a monster is directly harmed by the closing portculis
           or by the retracting drawbridge itself; otherwise not.
C343-140 - Sort of.  As best I can tell, their hunger gets changed
           and the corpse is removed as soon as they start eating,
           no matter how large the corpse is, so the only ongoing
           effect is that they can't move (because they're busy
           eating); thus, if they get hit with a sleep ray it has no
           effect on the eating.  Either that or a tame kitten can
           eat a fire giant corpse in one turn.
C343-141 - Confirmed.  Quaff an uncursed potion of levitation and
           use a numeric prefix to rest for 9999 turns.  You'll
           stop resting when you get hungry (or are approached by
           a monster or the other usual reasons), whether you're
           still levitating or not.
C343-142 - Unknown.  I assume the fact that they continue to treat
           the tile just inside the door specially is not a bug.
           (It really only identifies them if there isn't another
           monster hanging around with them.)  You can #chat to
           them, but that uses a turn (and then they move, and any
           other monster around might move, so you no longer know
           for sure which is the shopkeeper).  Whenever you enter
           the shop, they welcome you, but this only identifies them
           if they're the only monster in the shop.  A stethoscope
           will tell you that they're peaceful (unless they're not).
C343-143 - Unknown.
C343-144 - Confirmed with black puddings.  Refers only to diagonal
           passages through actual rock; they can squeeze diagonally
           between boulders just fine.
C343-145 - Unknown or debatable.  I do find it odd that you can
           force a lock with such a large and cumbersome tool.
           I half expected the game to just tell me no.
C343-146 - Untested, but mbhit (in muse.c) calls doorlock (which is
           in lock.c), which returns true if anything happens, for
           example, if the door crashes open.  If doorlock returns
           true, mbhit immediately calls makeknown(), without
           checking cansee().  If monsters ever used wands of
           opening or locking, the same thing would happen.
C343-147 - Confirmed, I think.  I got a segfault; although it's hard
           to be absolutely certain of the exact cause, it did
           happen in a room full of magic traps when a purple worm
           tried to engulf me, and I was wearing = slow digestion.
